Tell each shell which surface it runs in: GHOSTTY_SURFACE_ID

Every surface (a tab or a split) already has a UUID, and Ghostty saves it
with the window state, so a restored surface gets the same UUID back.
The shell never saw it. Exporting it lets the shell find out what was
running in this surface before a restart, for example to offer to resume
a session there.

The variable is set when the surface is created, after the base
configuration is chosen, so a value inherited from a parent surface or
passed in through AppleScript or an App Intent never takes its place.

diff --git a/macos/Sources/Ghostty/Surface View/SurfaceView_AppKit.swift b/macos/Sources/Ghostty/Surface View/SurfaceView_AppKit.swift
index 3129acf..453bc19 100644
--- a/macos/Sources/Ghostty/Surface View/SurfaceView_AppKit.swift	
+++ b/macos/Sources/Ghostty/Surface View/SurfaceView_AppKit.swift	
@@ -390,7 +390,15 @@ extension Ghostty {
             ) { [weak self] event in self?.localEventHandler(event) }
 
             // Setup our surface. This will also initialize all the terminal IO.
-            let surface_cfg = baseConfig ?? SurfaceConfiguration()
+            var surface_cfg = baseConfig ?? SurfaceConfiguration()
+
+            // Tell the shell which surface it runs in. The id is saved and
+            // restored with the window state, so a restored surface sees the
+            // same value. Set after the base configuration is chosen, so a
+            // value inherited from a parent surface or passed in by a script
+            // never takes its place.
+            surface_cfg.environmentVariables["GHOSTTY_SURFACE_ID"] = id.uuidString
+
             let surface = surface_cfg.withCValue(view: self) { surface_cfg_c in
                 ghostty_surface_new(app, &surface_cfg_c)
             }
